    "title": "Bengals' Tee Higgins ranks among NFL's highest-graded WRs - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Duncan Day ,\n9 hours ago Bengals Tee Higgins (5) is stopped by the Jaguars defense during their game against the Jaguars at Paycor Stadium on Sunday October 4, 2026. The Jaguars won the game with a final score of 22-17. By Phil Didion/The Enquirer, USA TODAY Network via Reuters Co Tee Higgins’ hot start to the 2026 season is showing up in the advanced metrics.\nThe Cincinnati Bengals wide receiver owns a 91.3 overall grade from Pro Football Focus , which ranks third among qualified NFL wide receivers through four games. His 90.7 receiving grade sits fourth at the position.\nHiggins has backed up those grades with 25 receptions for 401 yards and one touchdown. He's averaging 16 yards per catch, with PFF also crediting him with 94 yards after the catch and only one drop.\nHis biggest performance came in Sunday’s 22-17 loss to the Jacksonville Jaguars. Higgins caught 11 passes for 157 yards, leading Cincinnati in both categories.\nThe performance came with a tough ending, however, with Higgins suffering an adductor injury late in the fourth quarter. Head coach Zac Taylor said Monday that Higgins is considered day-to-day, an encouraging designation as Cincinnati prepares to visit Miami in Week 5.\nHiggins’ availability takes on hefty importance with Ja’Marr Chase also dealing with a concussion.\nThe Bengals travel to face the Dolphins on Sunday at 1 p.m. ET before heading into their Week 6 bye.\nThis article originally appeared on Bengals Wire: Bengals’ Tee Higgins ranks among NFL’s highest-graded WRs",
    "summary": "By Phil Didion/The Enquirer, USA TODAY Network via Reuters Co Tee Higgins’ hot start to the 2026 season is showing up in the advanced metrics. Higgins caught 11 passes for 157 yards, leading Cincinnati in both categories. The performance came with a tough ending, however, with Higgins suffering an adductor injury late in the fourth quarter. Head coach Zac Taylor said Monday that Higgins is considered day-to-day, an encouraging designation as Cincinnati prepares to visit Miami in Week 5. Higgins’ availability takes on hefty importance with Ja’Marr Chase also dealing with a concussion.",
